<title>USA: Boll weevil seen early threat to West Texas cotton.</title>
<headline>Boll weevil seen early threat to West Texas cotton.</headline>
<byline>Suzanne Rostler</byline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-05-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Having spent the early part of the season battling rain and cold weather, West Texas cotton farmers are now gearing up to take on their most ferocious enemy: the boll weevil.</p>
<p>Preliminary tests on more than 3 million acres around the Texas High Plains, or 85 percent of the state's cotton crop, found extremely high numbers of the migratory, cotton-eating pests, Texas cotton industry officials said.</p>
<p>Traps set from April 1 to May 16 turned out 1,742 weevils, compared with 105 in the same period last year and 585 in 1995.</p>
</text>
